PROJECT LICENSING NOTICE — ETS2_Reverse_Planner

Current distribution profile: 0.1.0-beta; historical-MIT-code.
Historical scope audited here: v0.1.0-beta (independent project). ETS2 v0.6.0 is outside this notice and is not used to infer inheritance.

1. HISTORICAL ORIGINAL CODE
The full original license is reproduced without amendment in licenses/LICENSE.historical-MIT.txt. For code covered by that license at the historical versions listed above, its MIT permissions remain effective. The historical MIT grant covered the original project code within its then-applicable scope; it was NOT limited to third-party code. This notice neither revokes nor retroactively narrows those grants. The original license controls if a summary conflicts with it. No new blanket MIT grant for unidentified later additions is made by this index.

2. MODELS, TEXTURES AND OTHER ASSETS
Historical asset-license coverage is unresolved. This notice does not retroactively declare all assets MIT, and does not declare all assets proprietary. Existing lawful rights remain unaffected. Independent authorship, original grants and third-party/game-derived portions require file-level evidence before any future proprietary asset designation.

3. THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS
SCS SDK, Hans telemetry wrapper provenance, MinHook and HDE retain their original copyright and license notices in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md and licenses/. They do not establish a license for the entire project. ReversePlanner additionally retains ETS2LA and historical upstream-code grants.

4. FUTURE ORIGINAL CODE
A prospective proprietary license may govern only material for which the licensor holds the necessary rights, explicitly identified by file or portion, beginning at an approved NEW release version and immutable commit. Eligible original code includes previously MIT-published original code if the licensor holds full relicensing authority; eligibility is not limited to new additions. Third-party code lacking that authority is excluded. No proprietary cutover is active in this distribution. Earlier lawful MIT rights and third-party permissions survive any later cutover. Merely changing this notice, renaming a file or rebuilding a binary does not revoke earlier rights.
